  {
    "@id": "https://www.kingstheatre.ca/temporary/e7d28dd0-c641-42a4-8b0a-eb9231d3490c",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Event"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://www.kingstheatre.ca/new-events/the-final-recordings-of-an-almost-extinct-bird/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/eventAttendanceMode": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g7301760"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://kingstheatre.ca/events/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://www.kingstheatre.ca/kingweb/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/494302742_1267454042052547_2244218746655134312_n.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/startDate": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "2026-8-22T19:30-3:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/endDate": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "2026-8-22T23:59-3:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Final Recordings of an Almost Extinct Bird"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\n                “Final Recordings” is where TEDTalk, sob story, and cottagecore collide.\nFred has a lot on his mind. The origins of the universe, for starters. The fabric of space-time, too. The formation of the Earth, plate tectonics, the fossil record, gravitational waves, dark matter, winding valley roads, campfires, pottery cafes—the list goes on and on. There’s also the matter of the giant salty egg, the man in the comfy blue chair, and the burning question: “Is this good? What I’m doing? With my time?”\nRuntime: 90 minutes, no intermission.\n\nZuppa: Based in Kjipuktuk / Halifax, we aim to make extraordinary experiences that provoke the consideration of new perspectives and ideas and thrive on the play between the imagined and the real. With each project we attempt to do something we don’t know how to do and work with collaborators who bring new life experiences or skill sets to the table. We make shows for Halifax and for places far away, with the support of The Canada Council for the Arts, Arts Nova Scotia, and The Nova Scotia Department of Communities, Culture, Tourism and Heritage, and Halifax Culture and Community. Zuppa is a member of Theatre Nova Scotia and an affiliate member of the Professional Association of Canadian Theatres.\n\n\n CREDITS\n\nBen Stone* – director, playwright\nStewart Legere – performer, playwright, composer, sound designer\nChristian Ludwig Hansen – projection design\nJess Lewis – lighting and set design\nLeesa Hamilton – costume and set design\nBernie Francis – Mi’kmaw language consultant\nFundy Geological Museum, Regan Maloney – science consultant\nSophie Schade – producer\nMary Fay Coady – interim producer\nGaby Milner – FSPA student apprentice\n\n\n\n* The participation of Ben Stone is arranged by permission of Canadian Actors’ Equity Association under the provisions of the Dance•Opera•Theatre Policy.\n\n \n\nTICKETS:  \n\nAdults: $40\nSupported: $20\nYouth: $10\nHST Additional\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDoors open 45 minutes before showtime.\nKing’s Theatre is committed to accessibility. If price is a barrier to attending, please request a ‘Bursary Ticket’ at the Box Office.\nTickets are not refundable for cash, but can be exchanged for credit with a minimum of 48 hours notice. Credits are valid for 12 months.\nAll persons require a ticket; we do not allow babes in arms. Children under 12 must be accompanied by a guardian.\nOutside food and beverages may not be brought into the theatre.\nFor more information about Theatre policies, click here.\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n            "
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/eventStatus": [
      {
        "@id": "http://schema.org/EventScheduled"
      }
    ]
  },
